# Phase 18 continuation — 2026-10-21

- Continued pinned Java 5.5.1 `ProcessQueue`/`PopProcessQueue` review. Both use
  `rocketmq.client.pull.pullMaxIdleTime`, default `120000`, and strict signed
  `currentTimeMillis - lastPullTimestamp > timeout` semantics. Rust now parses
  the dotted process-environment property as a Java-compatible long at Push
  startup, uses one shared expiry helper for Push and POP queues, and reports
  invalid values through `PullIdleTimeSettings`.
- Added a third invoked Java `rebalanceRetention` case with a subscribed,
  assigned, expired queue. The first Rust comparison revealed both queue
  expiry and fixture behavior: the Java rebalance removes the expired queue,
  attempts to clear the dirty offset, then the fixture's unsupported offset
  read prevents replacement. The Rust test recording store now injects that
  read failure for this case, preserving the actual remove-then-add attempt;
  production rebalance was not changed to suppress additions. The corrected
  comparison matches table presence, dropped state, retained/persisted offset
  and exact per-queue event order.
- Added parser and exact-threshold/wrapping tests. An initial negative-timeout
  assertion used an elapsed value equal to the timeout and failed; corrected
  the test input to exercise the intended strict inequality. Final all-feature
  Consumer tests pass (231 unit tests plus all targets/doctests), strict
  all-target/all-feature Consumer Clippy passes, formatting and diff checks
  pass. Pinned `mvn -f compat/java-reference/pom.xml verify` passes, including
  consumer-operation fixture drift verification and the complete 37-scenario
  Java compatibility suite. Fixture SHA-256:
  `73e2e0e8514bbdc904c89a8bde71cfe5fcf8eae431b4f507e7c189a4dc8e62a2`.
- Updated Phase 6 configuration mapping, Phase 11 method matrix, and the
  Phase 18 runtime operation audit to document the property, expiry semantics,
  evidence, and remaining POP assignment/public API audit. Broad P18-V01,
  final workspace gate, and Phase 19 live evidence remain open.

# Phase 18 continuation — POP assignment retention — 2026-10-21

- Continued the RebalanceImpl assignment-source review for POP queues. Java
  removes an expired POP queue only during an authoritative assignment update;
  a dropped flag alone does not remove an assigned table entry. Rust previously
  removed expired/dropped queues even when assignment lookup failed, and its
  addition pass replaced a dropped-but-still-registered queue. Scoped cleanup
  to unsubscribed truncation or authoritative assignment exclusion/expiry, and
  kept Java's putIfAbsent behavior for already registered POP queues.
- Added `popRebalance` to the pinned Java runtime fixture. It reflectively
  invokes the actual `RebalanceImpl.updateMessageQueueAssignment` branch for
  expired-assigned replacement, dropped-assigned retention, and empty
  assignment removal. Rust's `broker_assignment_error_preserves_worker_until_empty_assignment`
  compares the fixture rows and scripts an unavailable assignment while the
  live replacement is expired, then verifies dropped-but-assigned retention
  until a later authoritative empty result. Fixture SHA-256:
  `3d7af953b38b28a742a0d3eeb2671e8484066203f06ade2c829e2485ead1de4f`.
- The first full Maven pass reached the consumer fixture but the new reflected
  branch had started a non-daemon `PullMessageServiceScheduledThread1`. A live
  `jcmd Thread.print` confirmed Maven was joining that worker. Fixture teardown
  now shuts down the pull message service explicitly; focused generation exits
  normally and the complete pinned `mvn -f compat/java-reference/pom.xml verify`
  passes (37-scenario manifest, Java fixture drift checks and cross-decodes).
- Consumer all-feature tests pass (231 unit tests plus all crate integration
  and documentation targets), strict all-target/all-feature Consumer Clippy,
  formatting and diff checks pass. Phase 13 evidence now records the chosen
  POP branches. Other POP assignment paths, public Rebalance/ProcessQueue API
  correspondence, broad P18-V01, final workspace gates and Phase 19 live
  evidence remain open.

