# Phase 18 continuation — concurrent expired-message cleanup — 2026-10-21

- Pinned Java 5.5.1 `ConsumeMessageConcurrentlyService` marks each listener
  message with `CONSUME_START_TIME` and schedules `ProcessQueue.cleanExpiredMsg`
  after one consume-timeout interval, repeating at that interval. Added the
  same start marker, queue-owned timestamp tracking and service-owned cleaner
  timer to Rust. Cleanup uses Java's strict signed-millisecond comparison,
  delay level 3, successful-send-back removal, the 16-attempt queue cap and
  orderly-consume skip. In-flight batch completion remains safe when cleanup
  removes an expired message.
- Added pinned Java direct `ProcessQueue.cleanExpiredMsg` fixtures for
  successful expiry, repeated send-back failure, orderly skip and retry cap.
  Rust comparisons also cover strict timeout boundary, expiry during a blocked
  listener callback, late completion and initial timer delay. Fixture SHA-256:
  `76b9bdfdd20d3f6cbc1b73f4b12055c484955f39cfde5884e0d6688e38fd6f79`.
